1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,561
An old village minister steps out of his front door at sunset and says a short prayer aloud.

2
00:00:05,561 --> 00:00:10,783
He does not ask for money, or rain, or for his enemies to be punished.

3
00:00:10,783 --> 00:00:17,645
He asks God to look kindly on every creature that is born to die, and to give his town one more day.

4
00:00:17,645 --> 00:00:21,254
Then he says good night to the sun: good-bye, but just for now.

5
00:00:26,733 --> 00:00:32,815
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 9: Every Morning I Wake, by Dylan Thomas.

6
00:00:32,815 --> 00:00:37,522
It is the gentlest poem in your course, and its gentleness is an argument.

7
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,008
Six parts.

8
00:00:41,008 --> 00:00:43,274
Dylan Thomas and Under Milk Wood.

9
00:00:43,274 --> 00:00:44,836
Morning and evening.

10
00:00:44,836 --> 00:00:46,529
Not wholly bad or good.

11
00:00:46,529 --> 00:00:48,118
Good-bye, but just for now.

12
00:00:48,118 --> 00:00:49,355
The themes.

13
00:00:49,355 --> 00:00:51,074
And the form and devices.

14
00:00:55,433 --> 00:01:03,312
Dylan Thomas was born in Swansea, in south Wales, in nineteen fourteen, and published his first book of poems at twenty.

15
00:01:03,312 --> 00:01:12,583
He was a poet of sound before sense, building lines out of alliteration and rhyme, and reading them in a famous deep voice.

16
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:14,940
He is often called a late Romantic.

17
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:21,712
He worked for the BBC from nineteen forty five, and died in New York in nineteen fifty three, aged thirty nine.

18
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:29,172
The poem comes from Under Milk Wood, a radio play first broadcast in nineteen fifty four.

19
00:01:29,172 --> 00:01:41,932
It follows one spring day in an invented Welsh seaside town called Llareggub, a name which, read backwards, is a rude joke: Thomas loves the place but will not sentimentalise it.

20
00:01:41,932 --> 00:01:51,515
The speaker is the Reverend Eli Jenkins, a gentle, poetry writing minister, who recites these verses at his door as the sun goes down.

21
00:01:51,515 --> 00:01:53,056
The title is just the first line.

22
00:01:57,833 --> 00:01:58,928
Stanza one.

23
00:01:58,928 --> 00:02:03,355
Every morning when I wake, dear Lord, a little prayer I make.

24
00:02:03,355 --> 00:02:09,553
Notice little: modest in length and ambition, said daily, not in a crisis.

25
00:02:09,553 --> 00:02:13,277
And Dear Lord, the way you start a letter to someone you know.

26
00:02:13,277 --> 00:02:20,230
The request is startlingly small: that God keep his loving eye on all poor creatures born to die.

27
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:23,499
Not to do anything; just to look, with love.

28
00:02:23,499 --> 00:02:29,254
Poor means pitiable, and creatures means everything created, animals included.

29
00:02:29,254 --> 00:02:31,723
The only qualification is that we are mortal.

30
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:35,174
Stanza two mirrors the first.

31
00:02:35,174 --> 00:02:42,505
Every morning becomes every evening, and the prayer for all creatures becomes a blessing on one small town.

32
00:02:42,505 --> 00:02:46,372
The poem has the shape of a day, and of the whole play.

33
00:02:46,372 --> 00:02:51,242
Then the frankest line: whether they last the night is always touch and go.

34
00:02:51,242 --> 00:02:53,130
It means highly uncertain.

35
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,153
It is an astonishingly plain thing for a clergyman to say.

36
00:02:57,153 --> 00:03:01,206
He promises no protection; faith does not remove the risk.

37
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,739
Stanza three holds the poem's most famous line.

38
00:03:09,739 --> 00:03:14,035
We are not wholly bad or good, who live our lives under Milk Wood.

39
00:03:14,035 --> 00:03:24,608
The town in the play has a man who dreams of poisoning his wife, a woman who scrubs her house all day, a blind old sea captain in love with the drowned.

40
00:03:24,608 --> 00:03:26,809
None is a saint, none a villain.

41
00:03:26,809 --> 00:03:30,077
Thomas puts all of humanity in the middle.

42
00:03:30,077 --> 00:03:39,267
The figure is litotes, understatement by denying the opposite: saying not wholly bad admits the badness first, and is warmer for it.

43
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:47,728
Then he tells God what God will do: You, I know, will be the first to see our best side, not our worst.

44
00:03:47,728 --> 00:03:48,991
Three points.

45
00:03:48,991 --> 00:03:58,588
I know, not I hope: the only moment of complete confidence, and it is confidence in God's generosity, not in his own goodness.

46
00:03:58,588 --> 00:04:05,137
The first: neighbours and gossips see the worst first; God will be first to look for the best.

47
00:04:05,137 --> 00:04:11,223
And Thou and wilt, the old intimate form of the chapel prayer book, affectionate rather than grand.

48
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,960
The last stanza is the only one that asks for something concrete, and what it asks for is simply more time: O let us see another day!

49
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:28,436
The exclamation mark is the loudest sound in the poem.

50
00:04:28,436 --> 00:04:35,082
Then the whole town, in his imagination, bows to the setting sun and says good-bye, but just for now.

51
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,333
That phrase does two jobs.

52
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:41,838
On the surface it is simple fact: the sun will be back in the morning.

53
00:04:41,838 --> 00:04:46,916
Underneath, it answers the first stanza's all poor creatures born to die.

54
00:04:46,916 --> 00:04:51,786
If good night to the sun is only a short parting, perhaps death is too.

55
00:04:51,786 --> 00:04:59,203
The poem never says so; it lets a cheerful, throwaway phrase carry a hope it is too modest to state.

56
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:18,793
The central idea: a minister's daily prayer asks not for protection or wealth but for mercy, that God look lovingly on mortal creatures, see that ordinary people are mixed rather than wicked, and grant them one more day.

57
00:05:18,793 --> 00:05:22,270
Themes: prayer as a habit of ordinary days.

58
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:26,723
Human imperfection, neither sentimental nor puritanical.

59
00:05:26,723 --> 00:05:28,976
Mortality without terror.

60
00:05:28,976 --> 00:05:31,650
And a merciful God, imagined humbly.

61
00:05:32,867 --> 00:05:39,171
The structure is a narrowing circle: from all creation, to one town, to one night.

62
00:05:39,171 --> 00:05:42,738
Then the last line opens it out again to the sun.

63
00:05:42,738 --> 00:05:45,130
The whole poem is one turn of the earth.

64
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:50,774
The form.

65
00:05:50,774 --> 00:06:00,136
Sixteen lines in four quatrains, each built from two rhyming couplets: a a b b, c c d d, and so on.

66
00:06:00,136 --> 00:06:08,105
All eight rhymes are perfect, and a couplet is the most settled rhyme in English: the ear gets what it is waiting for at once.

67
00:06:08,105 --> 00:06:14,941
So in a poem about uncertainty and death, the sound keeps promising what the sense cannot.

68
00:06:14,941 --> 00:06:19,994
The metre is mostly iambic tetrameter, the beat of hymns and ballads.

69
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:22,699
Devices to quote.

70
00:06:22,699 --> 00:06:26,150
Apostrophe: the poem is spoken to God.

71
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:29,131
Litotes: not wholly bad or good.

72
00:06:29,131 --> 00:06:31,019
The idiom touch and go.

73
00:06:31,019 --> 00:06:34,366
Parallelism: every morning, every evening.

74
00:06:34,366 --> 00:06:37,790
The setting sun as a symbol of death and return.

75
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:40,984
And exclamations of cheer at the very edge of death.

76
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:44,170
Pause and think.

77
00:06:51,467 --> 00:06:56,308
Eli Jenkins, the village minister, recites it at his door at sunset.

78
00:06:58,300 --> 00:06:58,735
One more.

79
00:07:06,367 --> 00:07:11,837
Rhyming couplets, every rhyme perfect: settled sound for an unsettled subject.

80
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:15,115
Quick recap.

81
00:07:15,115 --> 00:07:17,003
Thomas and Under Milk Wood.

82
00:07:17,003 --> 00:07:20,050
A little prayer for all creatures born to die.

83
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:22,824
A town whose every night is touch and go.

84
00:07:22,824 --> 00:07:24,881
People not wholly bad or good.

85
00:07:24,881 --> 00:07:27,682
And a cheerful good-bye to the sun, just for now.

86
00:07:31,867 --> 00:07:33,783
That was Every Morning I Wake.

87
00:07:33,783 --> 00:07:37,415
Next: I Was My Own Route, by Julia de Burgos.

88
00:07:37,415 --> 00:07:37,976
See you there.

